{
  "regeln": [
    "Open each video with a sharp visual hook that directly illustrates the core commercial conflict of the story.",
    "Ensure every visual motif specifically depicts the exact individual, company, or concrete transaction mentioned in the narration rather than relying on generic workplace metaphors.",
    "All narrated facts, dates, figures and quotations must be verifiable from reliable sources; never present unverified claims.",
    "Match every visual motif (illustration, photo, animation) to the specific spoken business milestone or action it describes; avoid generic or unrelated stock images.",
    "Use at least 18 genuinely distinct, source‑backed visual motifs in every 35‑45 second Short, each lasting 3‑5 seconds and reflecting a different subject, action, or detail.",
    "Maintain precise synchronization of narration, subtitles, and visual changes throughout the video.",
    "Apply format‑specific pacing: rapid conflict beats for Shorts and deliberate, detailed arcs for long‑form videos; do not impose one format’s pacing on the other.",
    "Introduce the correct channel presenter at the start and bring them back in useful, story‑relevant actions; never replace them with unrelated characters.",
    "Conclude business narratives with a clear, definitive resolution of the central commercial conflict.",
    "When audience retention dips are observed, treat them as provisional feedback and test isolated narrative or visual adjustments rather than assuming unobserved causes.",
    "Preserve the channel’s signature painted‑world aesthetic and recurring presenter identity while varying settings and actions to keep the style fresh.",
    "Do not overgeneralize a single defect into a blanket ban on a style; phrase lessons conditionally and base new rules only on recurring, observable issues."
  ],
